he standard solution. Operating conditions— Detector: An ultraviolet absorption photometer (wave-length: 254 nm). MS: Amount [mg (potency)] of Cefroxadine RS taken Internal standard solution—Dissolve 1.6 g of vanillin in 5 mL of methanol, and add a mixture of dilute acetic acid and phosphoric acid (500:1) to make 100 mL. Operating conditions— Detector: An ultraviolet absorption photometer (wave-length: 254 nm). Column: A stainless steel column 4.6 mm in inside diame-ter and 10 cm in length, packed with octadecylsilanized silica gel for liquid chromatography (5 mm in particle diameter). Column temperature: A constant temperature of about 259C. Mobile phase: A mixture of a solution of ammonium sul-fate (1 in 50) and acetonitrile (97:3). Flow rate: Adjust so that the retention time of cefroxadine is about 10 minutes. System suitability— System performance: When the procedure is run with 10 mL of the standard solution under the above operating con-ditions, cefroxadine and the internal standard are eluted in this order with the resolution between these peaks being not ‌700 less than 1.5. System repeatability: When the test is repeated 6 times with 10 mL of the standard solution under the above operat-ing conditions, the relative standard deviation of the ratios of the peak areas of cefroxadine to that of the internal stand-ard is not more than 1.0z."
