      "appearance": {
        "en": "Isoxsuprine Hydrochloride occurs as a white, Test for required detectability: Pipet 1 mL of the standard ‌1210 solution, and add the mobile phase to make exactly 10 mL. Confirm that the peak area of isoxsuprine obtained with 10 mL of this solution is equivalent to 7 to 13z of that with 10 mL of the standard solution. System performance: To 1 mL of the sample solution add 2.5 mL of a solution of methyl parahydroxybenzoate (1 in 25,000) and the mobile phase to make 50 mL. When the procedure is run with 10 mL of this solution under the above operating conditions, methyl parahydroxybenzoate and isox-suprine are eluted in this order with the resolution between these peaks being not less than 4. System repeatability: When the test is repeated 6 times with 10 mL of the standard solution under the above operat-ing conditions, the relative standard deviation of the peak MS: Amount (mg) of isoxsuprine hydrochloride for assay taken Dissolution <6.10> When the test is performed at 50 revolu-tions per minute according to the Paddle method, using 900 mL of water as the dissolution medium, the dissolution rate in 15 minutes of Isoxsuprine Hydrochloride Tablets is not less than 80z. Start the test with 1 tablet of Isoxsuprine Hydrochloride Tablets, withdraw not less than 20 mL of the medium at the specified minute after starting the test, and filter through a membrane filter with a pore size not exceeding 0.45 mm. Dis-card not less than 10 mL of the first filtrate, pipet V mL of the subsequent filtrate, add water to make exactly V? mL so that each mL contains about 11 mg of isoxsuprine hydrochlo- area of isoxsuprine is not more than 2.5z. ride (C 18H 23NO3.HCl), and use this solution as the sample Loss on drying <2.41> Not more than 0.5z (1 g, 1059C, 1 hour). Residue on ignition <2.44> Not more than 0.2z (1 g)."
      },
      "testSpecification": [
        {
          "@type": "IdentificationSpecification",
          "testName": {
            "en": "Identification"
